Original commit body from f6e2b95fb282510495c8f9ffb1a10339e21e9431:

``csharp.py`` stamped ``meta["call_construct"] = "method_group"`` on the two
``references`` edges it emits for a C# method group (``Action h = Handle;``,
``items.ForEach(Process)``). A method group REFERENCES a method without
invoking it — the ``edge_type="references"`` was already correct; the meta KEY
was wrong.

These two sites were the only place in the tree where the two sibling keys
actually overlapped, against a declaration (``ref_construct``'s MetaKeySpec)
that says they have distinct edge families. Found by the Fundamental Concept
Audit of ``call_construct`` via an AST check asserting every producer site sits
on an edge type within the key's declared scope; the manual sweep had missed
them, which is the audit playbook's own prediction about manual sweeps.

``method_group`` joins ``ref_construct``'s documented value space, alongside
the other reference-family constructs (jsx, script_src, view_render, ...).

NO CONSUMER IS AFFECTED, and this is checkable rather than hopeful: every
reader of ``call_construct`` in the tree tests it against the single literal
``"method"`` (io_boundary.py:507 and :874, taint.py:1891,
verify_claims.py:1755), and ``method_group`` had no reader at all.

Tests follow the INV-vakaf precedent — assert the NEW key present AND the OLD
key absent, so a partial migration that writes both cannot pass. The two
cross-file cases previously asserted only an edge count; they now assert the
meta as well, since an edge count alone would not have caught this defect.

